<subsection> 
 <title>Formulas for one proportion</title>
<p>
<ul>
<li><m>\pi</m> is the underlying process probability of success or population proportion of success.</li>
<li><m>n</m> is the sample size.</li>
<li><m>\hat{p} = \frac{\text{number of successes in the sample}}{n}</m> is the sample proportion.</li>
<li><m>SD(\hat{p})</m> is the standard deviation of the statistic.</li>
<li><m>SD = \sqrt{ \pi(1-\pi) / n } </m> is the theory-based standard deviation of the null distribution.</li>
<li><m>SE = \sqrt{ \hat{p}(1-\hat{p}) / n } </m> is the theory-based standard error, which approximates <m>SD</m> and <m>SD(\hat{p})</m>.</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li><m>\text{standardized statistic} = \frac{\text{statistic} - \text{mean of the null distribution}}{\text{SD of the null distribution}} </m> is the number of standard deviations away from the mean of the sampling distribution a statistic is.</li>
<ul><li><m> z = \frac{\hat{p} - \pi}{\text{Simulated SD of null distribution}} </m> is the simulation-based standardized statistic for one proportion.</li>
<li><m> z = \frac{\hat{p} - \pi}{SD}  =  \frac{\hat{p} - \pi}{\sqrt{ \pi(1-\pi) / n } } </m> is the theory-based standardized statistic for one proportion.</li></ul>
</ul>
<ul><li><m> z = \frac{\hat{p} - \pi}{\text{Simulated SD of null distribution}} </m> is the simulation-based standardized statistic for one proportion.</li>
<li><m> z = \frac{\hat{p} - \pi}{SD}  =  \frac{\hat{p} - \pi}{\sqrt{ \pi(1-\pi) / n } } </m> is the theory-based standardized statistic for one proportion.</li></ul>
<ul>
<li><m>\text{statistic} \pm \text{margin of error} =  \text{statistic} \pm \text{(multiplier)} \times \text{(SD of statistic)}</m> is a confidence interval.</li>
<ul><li><m>\hat{p} \pm 2 \times SD(\hat{p}) </m> is a 2SD CI approximating a 95% confidence interval estimating <m>\pi</m>.</li>
<li><m>\hat{p} \pm \text{(multiplier)} \times SE </m> is a theory-based confidence interval estimating <m>\pi</m>, where the multiplier comes from the standard normal distribution.</li></ul>
</ul>
<ul><li><m>\hat{p} \pm 2 \times SD(\hat{p}) </m> is a 2SD CI approximating a 95% confidence interval estimating <m>\pi</m>.</li>
<li><m>\hat{p} \pm \text{(multiplier)} \times SE </m> is a theory-based confidence interval estimating <m>\pi</m>, where the multiplier comes from the standard normal distribution.</li></ul>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection> 
 <title>Formulas for one mean</title>
<p>
<ul>
<li><m>\mu</m> is the population mean.</li>
<li><m>\sigma</m> is the population standard deviation.</li>
<li><m>n</m> is the sample size.</li>
<li><m>\overline{x}</m> is the sample mean.</li>
<li><m>s</m> is the sample standard deviation.</li>
<li><m>SD(\overline{x})</m> is the standard deviation of the statistic.</li>
<li><m>SD = \sigma/\sqrt{n} </m> is the theory-based standard deviation of the null distribution.</li>
<li><m>SE = s / \sqrt{n} </m> is the theory-based standard error, which approximates <m>SD</m> and <m>SD(\overline{x})</m>.</li></ul>
<ul>
<li><m>\text{standardized statistic} = \frac{\text{statistic} - \text{mean of the null distribution}}{\text{SD of the null distribution}} </m> is the number of standard deviations away from the mean of the sampling distribution a statistic is.</li>
<ul><li><m> t = \frac{\overline{x} - \mu}{\text{Simulated SD of null distribution}} </m> is the simulation-based standardized statistic for one mean.</li>
<li><m> t = \frac{\overline{x} - \mu}{SE} =  \frac{\overline{x} - \mu}{s/\sqrt{n}} </m> is the theory-based standardized statistic for one mean.</li></ul>
</ul>
<ul><li><m> t = \frac{\overline{x} - \mu}{\text{Simulated SD of null distribution}} </m> is the simulation-based standardized statistic for one mean.</li>
<li><m> t = \frac{\overline{x} - \mu}{SE} =  \frac{\overline{x} - \mu}{s/\sqrt{n}} </m> is the theory-based standardized statistic for one mean.</li></ul>
<ul>
<li><m>\text{statistic} \pm \text{margin of error} =  \text{statistic} \pm \text{(multiplier)} \times \text{(SD of statistic)}</m> is a confidence interval.</li>
<ul><li><m>\overline{x} \pm 2 \times SD(\overline{x}) </m> is a 2SD CI approximating a 95% confidence interval estimating <m>\mu</m>.</li>
<li><m>\overline{x} \pm \text{(multiplier)} \times SE </m> is a theory-based confidence interval estimating <m>\mu</m>, where the multiplier comes from the t-distribution with <m>n-1</m> degrees of freedom.</li></ul>
</ul>
<ul><li><m>\overline{x} \pm 2 \times SD(\overline{x}) </m> is a 2SD CI approximating a 95% confidence interval estimating <m>\mu</m>.</li>
<li><m>\overline{x} \pm \text{(multiplier)} \times SE </m> is a theory-based confidence interval estimating <m>\mu</m>, where the multiplier comes from the t-distribution with <m>n-1</m> degrees of freedom.</li></ul>
</p>
</subsection>

</section>